## Top 5 Pitfalls Web & Backend Engineers Must Avoid

:::caution[Security & Stability Pitfalls]
* **1. Never Hardcode API Keys or Secrets:** Client application binaries are public assets. Anyone can decompile an `.apk` or `.ipa` package and extract string constants. Store tokens exclusively in KeyStore/Keychain or resolve them at runtime via OAuth SSO.
* **2. Never Run Heavy Loops on the Main Thread:** Long computations, synchronous file I/O, or parsing large JSON payloads freeze the screen, triggering OS crashes. Always dispatch work to background workers or coroutine dispatchers.
* **3. Never Assume Continuous Connectivity:** Always handle offline states, network timeouts, and flaky cellular signals. Provide local caching and retry mechanisms.
* **4. Clean Up Event Listeners to Prevent Memory Leaks:** Mobile OS devices have constrained RAM. Dangling event subscribers, uncancelled timers, and retained singleton delegates keep screens in memory, causing OOM (Out-of-Memory) termination.
* **5. Never Build Production Binaries on Personal Laptops:** Official application packages must be built, signed, and distributed exclusively through automated Bitrise CI pipelines to guarantee tamper-proof audit trails.
:::

